---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Lean body mass is body weight with the fat mass removed.
      The estimate uses height, weight and sex, and it shows three formulas at once.
      For example, a man of 80 kg and 180 cm gets 61.4 kg from Boer and 62.7 kg from James.
      Hume returns 57.8 kg for that man, so the three do not collapse into one number.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Lean Body Mass Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="what-lean-body-mass-is">
    <h2>What is lean body mass? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Lean body mass is body weight with the fat removed: muscle, bone, organs and water.
      A scale reports one number, so it cannot separate those parts.
      The split matters because fat loss and muscle loss look the same on that scale.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fat is not measured here.
      The numbers come from height and weight, plus sex.
      Treat the figure as an anchor, not as a scan.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="why-muscle-is-invisible">
    <h2>Why do the formulas ignore muscle? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The formulas ignore muscle because they never see anything beyond height, weight and sex.
      Clinicians wanted a lean mass figure when a scan was not in the room.
    </p>
    <p>
      Height, weight and sex were the inputs already on the chart, so the equations stop there.
      Two people of the same size therefore share one estimate, however different their training is.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="boer-james-hume">
    <h2>What is the difference between Boer, James and Hume? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The difference between Boer, James and Hume is the equation each author published, not a second reading of you.
      Boer from 1984 is linear, and it is the clinical default in the hero card.
      James from 1976 squares a weight-to-height ratio and leaves height in centimetres.
      Hume from 1966 is a second linear fit from a different sample.
    </p>
    <p>
      Comparing Boer, James, and Hume before writing a lean mass figure down is why all three stay visible.
      The James formula is the 1976 card. Fat free mass is another name for the same estimate, and an LBM calculator is the search that lands on this page.
      None of them is a lab result, and none of them is a dose.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="worked-numbers">
    <h2>How does each formula turn height and weight into kilograms? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Each formula turns height and weight into kilograms, and James keeps that height in centimetres.
      Boer multiplies a man's weight by 0.407 and his height by 0.267, then subtracts 19.2.
    </p>
    <p>
      On 80 kg and 180 cm that is 32.56 plus 48.06, minus 19.2.
      The result is 61.42 kg, and the page shows 61.4 kg.
    </p>
    <p>
      James starts from 1.10 times the weight, then subtracts 128 times the square of weight over height.
      For this man the subtraction leaves 62.7 kg, with height still in centimetres.
      Hume multiplies weight by 0.32810 and height by 0.33929, then subtracts 29.5336.
      The same man lands at 57.8 kg, about 4.9 kg under James.
    </p>
    <p>
      Women use different coefficients in all three equations.
      Boer for a woman is 0.252 times weight plus 0.473 times height, minus 48.3.
      The same 80 kg and 180 cm then gives 57.0 kg from Boer, not 61.4 kg.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="height-in-meters">
    <h2>What goes wrong if height is entered in meters? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Entering height in meters instead of centimetres shrinks every height term and breaks the formulas.
      The field wants centimetres, so 180 cm typed as 1.8 is a height of 1.8 cm.
    </p>
    <p>
      James divides weight by that tiny height and the lean mass falls below zero.
      The card then holds the figure inside the body weight and says so.
    </p>
    <p>
      Boer for an 80 kg man at 1.8 cm falls to about 13.8 kg, against 61.4 kg at 180 cm.
      The spread warning should fire, because the formulas no longer describe one body.
      Retype the height in centimetres before you trust any of the three numbers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="not-a-dose">
    <h2>What if one formula is copied into a dose? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Someone copying one formula into a drug dose treats an estimate as if it were a measured fact.
      At 120 kg and 170 cm, Boer is about 75.0 kg and Hume is about 67.5 kg.
      The gap is about 7.5 kg, which is wide enough to matter on a chart.
      This calculator does not produce a dose.
    </p>
    <p>
      Checking the kilogram gap before a formula is copied into a chart takes one look at the spread.
      A clinician has to answer any dosing question, since the calculator will not.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="which-formula">
    <h2>Which formula should you use? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Use the Boer formula as the clinical default, and keep James and Hume on the page with it.
      No equation here measures you, and the dates are 1984, 1976 and 1966.
      Read the spread between the highest and the lowest before you copy a number.
    </p>
    <p>
      Estimating how much of a scale weight the formulas treat as lean is what the fat line is for.
      Implied fat uses Boer: weight minus that lean mass, divided by weight.
      It is not a body fat test. A tape-measure tool is the one that starts from circumferences.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Height in meters">
      <p>
        Entering height in meters instead of centimetres is the mistake that breaks the arithmetic.
        A height of 1.8 is read as 1.8 cm, not as 180 cm.
        Boer then falls to about 13.8 kg for an 80 kg man, and James falls below zero.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="A formula copied into a dose">
      <p>
        Copying one formula into a drug dose treats three estimates as one measured fact.
        On a 120 kg man at 170 cm, Boer and Hume differ by about 7.5 kg.
        Do not treat either number as a dose. Nothing here turns the estimate into one.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="One unlabeled number">
      <p>
        Reading one unlabeled number as the lean mass hides which formula was copied.
        Boer, James and Hume stay named so the source of each kilogram is obvious.
        The spread sits beside them, and that gap is part of the answer.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="The spread is part of the answer">
      <p>
        Keep Boer, James and Hume labeled, because the spread is part of the answer.
        A single number with no formula name is how a several-kilogram gap disappears.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>

    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="An estimate, on your device">
      <p>
        Runs entirely on your device. Nothing is uploaded.
        These figures are an estimate, not a measurement and not a dose.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
